# Lockfile Proxy Stripping

Local installs may route through a private npm proxy/registry, which bakes the
proxy URL into every package entry of **both** lockfiles. That host-specific URL
must never land in a PR: Vercel builds with `npm install` from
`package-lock.json` and cannot reach the proxy.

## Before Creating Any PR

1. **Always** strip proxy URLs from `bun.lock` and `package-lock.json` first.
2. Do **not** match on a specific host name — normalize **any** npm registry
   tarball URL.
3. Stage the cleaned lockfiles before opening the PR.

## What to Strip

### `bun.lock`

Each package entry is an array of the form:

```
"<key>": ["<name>@<version>", "<resolution>", { ...deps }, "<integrity>"]
```

The **second element** is the resolution/proxy field. When it points at a
registry tarball it must be reset to an empty string (the value Bun uses for
the default registry).

- With proxy:

```
"readdirp/picomatch": ["picomatch@2.3.2", "https://proxy.npm.your-company.com/picomatch/-/picomatch-2.3.2.tgz", {}, "sha512-V7+vQEJ06Z+c5tSye8S+nHUfI51xoXIXjHQ99cQtKUkQqqO1kO/KCJUfZXuB47h/YBlDhah2H3hdUGXn8ie0oA=="]
```

- After stripping:

```
"readdirp/picomatch": ["picomatch@2.3.2", "", {}, "sha512-V7+vQEJ06Z+c5tSye8S+nHUfI51xoXIXjHQ99cQtKUkQqqO1kO/KCJUfZXuB47h/YBlDhah2H3hdUGXn8ie0oA=="]
```

### `package-lock.json`

Each `packages` entry has a `resolved` tarball URL. When it points at a proxy,
it is rewritten to the public npm registry — exactly what npm writes with the
default registry. npm's default `replace-registry-host=npmjs` maps it back to
the configured registry on local installs, so proxied machines still work.

- With proxy:

```
"resolved": "https://proxy.npm.your-company.com/@adobe/css-tools/-/css-tools-4.5.0.tgz",
```

- After stripping:

```
"resolved": "https://registry.npmjs.org/@adobe/css-tools/-/css-tools-4.5.0.tgz",
```

## How to Strip

Use the project's TypeScript script (run by Bun). It is host-agnostic: it
matches any `http(s)` registry tarball URL (`.../-/<name>-<version>.tgz`) and
only rewrites that URL. Git, GitHub, file, and already-clean resolutions are
left untouched.

```bash
bun run strip-lock-proxy
```

The logic lives in [`scripts/strip-bun-lock-proxy.ts`](../../scripts/strip-bun-lock-proxy.ts).

Verify nothing remains before committing (exits non-zero if any proxy URL is
still present in either lockfile, so it doubles as a CI / pre-PR gate):

```bash
bun run strip-lock-proxy:check
```

## Automated via Git Hook

A committed pre-commit hook strips both lockfiles whenever either is staged,
and blocks the commit if `package-lock.json` is out of sync with
`package.json`. It lives at [`.githooks/pre-commit`](../../.githooks/pre-commit)
and delegates to any globally-configured hooks first (e.g. anything set
globally on your machine) so those are preserved.

Activate it once per clone:

```bash
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
```

The hook is a safety net, not a replacement for the rule — still run the strip
and `:check` before opening a PR.

## Rules

- **Never open a PR with proxy URLs in `bun.lock` or `package-lock.json`.**
- Keep the strip logic host-agnostic — do not hardcode a proxy hostname.
- Only the resolution / `resolved` field is changed; names, versions,
  dependency maps, and integrity hashes are never modified.
- Keeping the lockfiles in sync with `package.json` is covered by
  [`lockfile-sync-rule.mdc`](lockfile-sync-rule.mdc).
